course: dictionary
lesson: 3584
title: Fibrin and how blood clots
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fibrin is a science word, but its context tells you a lot. «When you cut yourself, platelets stick together at the cut and threads of a protein called fibrin form a net that traps blood cells into a clot.» The sentence says fibrin is a protein that makes threads and a net.
explain: Test your idea by swapping it into the sentence: «threads of a stringy protein form a net that traps blood cells into a clot.» That still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. A protein is a substance your body makes and uses, and fibrin is one of them.
explain: Fibrin works as a team with platelets. Platelets arrive first and stick to the cut. Then fibrin threads weave across them. «When you graze your knee, threads of fibrin make a net that dries into a scab, a natural plaster under which new skin grows.»
fact: Fibrin is a stringy protein in the blood.
fact: Fibrin forms threads that make a net.
fact: The net of fibrin traps blood cells, and this makes a clot.
fact: A clot stops bleeding, and it dries into a scab.
fact: Platelets help start the clot, and fibrin strengthens it.
exercise: «When you cut yourself, threads of a protein called fibrin form a net.» What is fibrin? | A tough bone in your finger | A germ that causes infection | A type of blood cell that carries oxygen | *A stringy protein in the blood that forms a net to make a clot
exercise: Which word in the sentence tells you fibrin is a protein? Find and copy it. | *protein | cut | threads | yourself
exercise: «Platelets stick at the cut, and fibrin threads trap blood cells.» What does trap mean here? | Wash clean | Warm up | Let go and float away | *Catch and hold
test: «When you graze your knee, threads of fibrin make a net that dries into a scab.» What does fibrin do in this sentence? | Turns into skin straight away | *Makes a net of threads that helps form a clot | Cools the knee down | Makes a net of threads
test: Ben says fibrin is a kind of plaster from a shop. Why is he only partly right? | *A scab is a natural plaster made by the body, using fibrin threads | Fibrin is only found in cuts on the face | Fibrin is a plaster sold in shops | Fibrin is a type of bandage
exam: «Picking a scab can let germs in and leave a scar.» What does the sentence suggest about the scab? | *It should always be picked off as soon as it forms | It is a natural plaster that protects new skin, so picking it is harmful | It is made of fibrin and nothing else | It cannot be damaged
